WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.600
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.759
I was duly awed by your epic and encyclopedic
rant about punishments.

00:00:04.759 --> 00:00:08.420
And so, even though I still believe in my
heart that I do not owe a punishment, I'm

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:11.370
going to do one because data does not lie.

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:14.879
Well, sometimes it does, but it lies less
than your heart.

00:00:14.879 --> 00:00:16.640
That's what today's video is kind of about,
actually.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.810
Okay, Hank, so my favorite character in this
summer's Book Club pick, "Behind the Beautiful

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:24.420
Forevers", is Sunil the twelve-year-old garbage
picker who grew up in an orphanage, in fact

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:25.420
we're told,

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:28.680
"Though Sunil was not an orphan, he understood
that phrases like 'AIDS orphan', and 'when

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:32.800
I was the second hand woman to Mother Teresa'
helped Sister Paulette, the nun who ran the

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:36.600
Handmaids of the Blessed Trinity Children's
Home, get money from foreigners."

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:39.629
By the way, little recommendation, when you
have a four-year-old child, don't take books

00:00:39.629 --> 00:00:40.629
to the beach.

00:00:40.629 --> 00:00:43.980
So anyway, that's the first mention of charity
in Behind the Beautiful Forevers, and boy,

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:45.140
is it complicated.

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:49.290
Sunil is a non-orphan in an orphanage, Sister
Paulette lies to raise money, and we later

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:52.579
read that when the orphanage collects clothes
for the kids and then sells those clothes

00:00:52.579 --> 00:00:53.629
to pay for other stuff.

00:00:53.629 --> 00:00:56.149
But while Sunil isn't an orphan, he does need
the orphanage.

00:00:56.149 --> 00:00:58.089
Plus, he learns to read while he's there.

00:00:58.089 --> 00:01:02.229
And that's the kind of non-straightforward
story about charities, both domestic and international,

00:01:02.229 --> 00:01:05.250
that we rarely hear, especially from charities
themselves.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:07.400
Right, because they have to raise money, and
telling people,

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:10.870
"Ah, we think this is going to go well, it
isn't going to be perfect, but we suspect

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:14.870
it will be a net-positive, although there's
no way to know", that's a terrible fundraising

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:15.870
strategy.

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:18.340
But that's closer to the reality that we see
in Behind the Beautiful Forevers, like the

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:23.000
charity-administered school in Annawadi is
taught by a teenager and definitely sub-standard.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:27.110
And toward the end of the book we learn that
many charity-administered schools are outright

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:28.110
frauds.

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:31.350
When a new school opened in the Pink Temple
by the sewage lake, many children gravitated

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:32.350
to it.

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:35.130
But it closed as soon as the leader of the
non-profit had taken enough pictures of children

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:37.040
studying to secure government funds.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:41.360
And then there are, like, collaborations between
government and charity, which are also sometimes

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:42.360
problematic.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:45.390
Like, you'll remember that Annawadi's political
representative who is super corrupt keeps

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:48.100
getting public amenities, care of the charity
World Vision.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:51.250
You know, like public toilets and gutters
etcetera, and that's all good stuff, but it's

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:54.360
timed to the elections so that he can keep
his power.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:57.980
And so you could see it as these projects
like further institutionalizing the corrupt

00:01:57.980 --> 00:01:58.980
power system, right.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:02.240
But then again, good toilets are really important
for public health.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:03.240
It's complicated.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:06.310
Hank, this is all particularly interesting
to me right now because, as you watch this,

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:10.379
I am in East Africa learning about some of
the work that people there are doing with

00:02:10.379 --> 00:02:11.599
the help of the Gates Foundation.

00:02:11.599 --> 00:02:16.019
Now, the situation in East Africa is vastly
different from the situation in Annawadi,

00:02:16.019 --> 00:02:20.319
but around the world, including in our own
neighborhoods, we need to look at what charities

00:02:20.319 --> 00:02:22.230
do and how well they do it.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:25.239
Hank, you don't want to be giving to charity
simply because it makes you feel good or because

00:02:25.239 --> 00:02:27.209
it lessens some guilt that you have.

00:02:27.209 --> 00:02:29.260
We want to actually decrease World Suck.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:32.709
I apologize for splitting that infinitive
but I felt that it was necessary.

00:02:32.709 --> 00:02:36.680
When we talk about health and poverty, we
are talking about incredibly complicated problems

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:40.959
and yes, money can be misallocated, and it
can be used to bolster corrupt regimes, but

00:02:40.959 --> 00:02:44.400
we have lots of data -- links in the doobly-doo
-- showing that in the last twenty years,

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:49.629
when people have access to the right resources
and tools, they can transform the health and

00:02:49.629 --> 00:02:51.819
economic opportunities in their communities.

00:02:51.819 --> 00:02:56.209
Rising income, better food security, higher
literacy rates, lower infant mortality -- those

00:02:56.209 --> 00:02:58.730
aren't anecdotes and they are real progress.

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:02.090
Hank, one of the reasons I continue to reread
"Behind the Beautiful Forevers" is that it

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:04.450
doesn't contain any unambiguous victories.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:07.719
Not for the characters, certainly, but also
not for foreign aid or government spending

00:03:07.719 --> 00:03:09.910
or microfinance or charity.

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:13.730
Non-profit spending doesn't always work, partly
because not all non-profits are equally effective

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:16.440
and partly because no one succeeds all of
the time.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:20.310
I think when trying to figure out what does
work, data is probably ultimately a better

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:21.310
guide than anecdote.

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:23.230
But then again, data can also be manipulated.

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:26.889
Hank, we are either going to embrace complexity
or we're going to be lying to ourselves.

00:03:26.889 --> 00:03:28.980
That's what I took away from "Behind the Beautiful
Forevers".

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:31.760
I look forward to reading your thoughts in
comments when I return.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:34.900
Hank, I hope to learn a lot this week, and
I hope to listen a lot, but if I come home

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:38.889
with straightforward answers and simple solutions,
I am probably doing it wrong.

00:03:38.889 --> 00:03:42.720
Okay Hank, although I will be very jet-lagged,
I will see you on Friday.

